-- CISSP Domain 7 expansion, part B (from-scratch coverage): 7.4 foundational security operations concepts (job rotation timing mechanics, mandatory vacation with access suspension, dual control in operational scenarios, least privilege for service/automation accounts, break-glass emergency access, on-call rotation vs job rotation, log-review segregation, compensating controls for small teams, individual accountability, OLA vs SLA; SoD/job-rotation basics and the dual-control-vs-SoD vault/key-ceremony examples intentionally NOT restated, see ci-f-060/1207/ci-y-251 and ci-q-1206/1207), 7.5 apply resource protection (media library custody, secure offsite media transport, vendor-maintenance escort, RMA sanitization, spare/cold-spare hardware security, ESD handling, decommissioned-equipment holding areas, certificate of destruction, sanitized/synthetic test-dev data, portable-asset check-out; data remanence clear/purge/destroy methods intentionally NOT restated, see ci-f-016 and ci-q-010/013), 7.6 incident management phases (detection, response, mitigation, reporting, recovery, remediation, lessons learned; event/incident/breach terminology intentionally NOT restated, see ci-f-058), 7.7 operate and maintain detective and preventative measures (anti-malware EPP/EDR layers, application allowlisting/blocklisting, sandbox detonation, honeytokens, honeypot legal considerations, IDS/IPS signature and threat-intel upkeep, firewall rule-base review, fleet-wide quarantine; firewall/IDS-IPS/honeypot/DMZ/NAC architecture basics intentionally NOT restated, see ci-f-1112 and ci-q-1108/1114/1116).

insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- 7.4 foundational security operations concepts (ci-q-1600..1612)
('ci-q-1600','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',1,$q$A security team schedules job rotations for privileged administrators on a fixed, publicly known annual calendar, announced a year in advance. An auditor recommends switching to unannounced, randomly-timed rotations instead. Why is the unannounced approach more effective as a fraud-detection control?$q$,$q$["An employee concealing fraud or errors can prepare and hide evidence before a rotation they know is coming, so removing that advance warning improves detection", "Announced rotations are prohibited under most labor regulations", "Unannounced rotations eliminate the need for any other personnel security control", "Randomly-timed rotations cost less to administer than scheduled ones"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The detective value of job rotation depends on catching someone off guard before they can cover their tracks. A well-publicized, predictable rotation date gives a person concealing fraud or manipulated records ample time to clean up evidence beforehand, defeating the control's purpose. Randomizing and withholding the timing removes that preparation window, which is the entire point - it has nothing to do with legality, cost, or replacing other controls.$q$),
('ci-q-1601','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',2,$q$An organization requires that privileged database administrators take at least five consecutive business days of vacation each year. During that vacation, their standing administrative access is suspended rather than merely going unused. What is the security purpose of also suspending the access, instead of just requiring the time away?$q$,$q$["Simply being physically away does not stop the employee from remotely using still-active credentials to keep concealing manipulated records; someone else must actually operate in their place for irregularities to surface", "It reduces the organization's payroll costs during the vacation period", "It is required so the vacation counts toward annual leave accrual", "It ensures the administrator cannot be blamed for any incident that occurs while they are away"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Mandatory vacation only works as a fraud-detection control if someone else is forced to actually perform the role while the regular incumbent is gone, since that is what surfaces irregularities. If the administrator's credentials remain live, they can log in remotely during the "vacation" and keep concealing whatever they were hiding, defeating the entire purpose - so the access itself, not just physical presence, must be suspended.$q$),
('ci-q-1602','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',1,$q$A company requires that restoring a production database from backup can only be executed after two separate on-call engineers each independently authenticate and approve the restore command in the deployment tool; neither engineer's approval alone is sufficient. What operational control does this describe?$q$,$q$["Dual control (two-person integrity) applied to a high-impact operational action", "Job rotation applied to database administration", "Mandatory vacation applied to on-call engineers", "Least privilege applied to the deployment tool's configuration"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Dual control requires two authorized people to act together, at the same time, before one sensitive action is allowed to proceed - here, a destructive production database restore. It is unrelated to job rotation (which moves people through roles over time) and to mandatory vacation (which removes someone from a role temporarily); it is also a stronger requirement than simply narrowing what one account can do, which is what least privilege addresses.$q$),
('ci-q-1603','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',3,$q$A CI/CD pipeline's deployment service account currently holds full administrative rights across every cloud subscription so that no deployment ever fails due to a missing permission. A security review recommends narrowing the service account to only the specific resource types and actions each pipeline stage actually needs. What operational principle is being applied, and to what kind of subject?$q$,$q$["Least privilege, applied to a non-human (service/automation) account rather than a human user account", "Job rotation, since the account's permissions will change over time", "Mandatory vacation, since the account is temporarily disabled", "Separation of duties, since two accounts are now required instead of one"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Least privilege is not limited to human identities - an overprivileged automation or service account is a common and dangerous real-world risk, since a single compromised pipeline credential with broad standing rights can affect every resource it touches. Scoping the account down to exactly what each pipeline stage needs, and nothing more, is least privilege applied operationally to a non-human subject; it does not involve rotating roles, suspending access for time off, or splitting the work across a second account.$q$),
('ci-q-1604','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',2,$q$An organization vaults a rarely-used emergency administrative credential that grants full access to a critical system. The credential can only be checked out during a declared outage, triggers an immediate alert to the security team the moment it is checked out, and requires a mandatory review of what was done within 24 hours of use. What is this type of account called?$q$,$q$["A break-glass (emergency access) account", "A shared service account", "A honeypot account", "A default vendor administrator account"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A break-glass account exists specifically for rare, urgent situations where normal access request processes would be too slow; because it grants powerful standing capability the moment it is used, it is compensated for with real-time alerting on checkout and a mandatory post-use review to confirm the access was appropriate - exactly the pattern described. A shared service account, a honeypot, and a default vendor account each serve entirely different purposes.$q$),
('ci-q-1605','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',1,$q$A security operations team rotates which analyst carries the on-call pager each week, so that after-hours incident response responsibility is shared and no single analyst is perpetually on call. Is this the same control as job rotation used for fraud detection, and why or why not?$q$,$q$["No - on-call rotation exists to distribute operational workload and ensure coverage, while job-rotation-as-fraud-control exists to periodically place a different person into a role specifically to expose concealed wrongdoing", "Yes - both are the exact same control simply applied to different job titles", "No - on-call rotation is a physical security control and has nothing to do with personnel security", "Yes - both controls only work if combined with mandatory vacation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Two controls can look superficially similar - people taking turns in a role - while serving completely different purposes. On-call rotation is about staffing coverage and preventing burnout; job rotation as a fraud-detection control is deliberately designed so a different set of eyes periodically reviews or performs a sensitive function, increasing the odds that concealed manipulation gets noticed. Confusing the two on the exam is a common trap, since neither substitutes for the other.$q$),
('ci-q-1606','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',2,$q$A firewall administrator configures and maintains all firewall rules. To ensure irregular or unauthorized rule changes are caught, a separate security analyst who has no ability to modify firewall rules is assigned to periodically review the firewall's change logs. What principle does assigning a different, non-administering person to review the logs reflect?$q$,$q$["Separation of duties between the person who performs an administrative action and the person who independently reviews it after the fact", "Least privilege, since the analyst simply has fewer rights than the administrator", "Dual control, since both people must act together to make each change", "Job rotation, since the analyst previously held the administrator's role"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Having someone other than the person performing an action also independently review that action's records is a form of separation of duties: the ability to act and the ability to audit that action are held by different people, so a single individual cannot both make an unauthorized change and be the one who checks for it. This differs from dual control, which requires both people to act together at the same moment rather than one reviewing the other's work afterward.$q$),
('ci-q-1607','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',3,$q$A five-person IT department cannot fully separate the roles of requesting, approving, and implementing system changes, because there simply are not enough staff to keep every stage performed by a different person. Which combination of compensating controls best offsets the resulting fraud/error risk?$q$,$q$["Increased management review of change logs, periodic rotation of who performs each stage among the available staff, and independent audit of completed changes", "Eliminating change review entirely, since full separation is impossible anyway", "Hiring exactly one additional employee, which by itself always fully resolves the gap", "Requiring every change to occur only during a staff member's mandatory vacation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$When an organization is too small to achieve textbook separation of duties, security relies on compensating controls that make undetected abuse harder even without a fully separated workflow: closer management oversight, rotating who fills each role so the same person is not permanently both requester and implementer, and independent audits that catch problems after the fact. Abandoning review, assuming one new hire magically fixes the structural gap, or scheduling changes around vacations does not address the underlying risk.$q$),
('ci-q-1608','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',1,$q$Three system administrators all log in to a critical server using the same shared "admin" account and password. After a suspicious configuration change is discovered, investigators cannot determine which administrator actually made it. What foundational operational practice was violated?$q$,$q$["Individual accountability for privileged actions - each administrator should use their own uniquely attributable credentials rather than a shared account", "Mandatory vacation", "Data classification", "Change advisory board approval"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Individual accountability requires that every privileged action can be traced back to the specific person who performed it. A shared account and password destroys that traceability - anyone with the shared password could have made the change, and no audit log entry can distinguish between them - which is precisely why privileged access should always be tied to a unique, personally attributable identity rather than a generic shared one.$q$),
('ci-q-1609','cissp',7,'7.4','mcq',2,$q$A company's external Service Level Agreement (SLA) with customers promises that reported security incidents will be acknowledged within one hour. To make that promise achievable, the internal security operations team and the network operations team agree between themselves that the network team will escalate any suspected incident to the SOC within ten minutes of detection. What is this internal, team-to-team agreement called?$q$,$q$["An Operational Level Agreement (OLA) - an internal commitment between teams that underpins an externally facing SLA", "A second SLA, since SLAs can also exist between purely internal teams", "A non-disclosure agreement", "A business associate agreement"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$An OLA is the internal counterpart to an external SLA: it defines what one internal team commits to provide another internal team so that the organization as a whole can meet its outward-facing commitments. The customer-facing promise (one-hour acknowledgment) is only achievable because the underlying internal handoff (ten-minute escalation) is fast enough to support it - a distinction the exam tests precisely because OLA and SLA are so easy to conflate.$q$),
('ci-q-1610','cissp',7,'7.4','multi',1,$q$Select the TWO practices that correctly apply least privilege in day-to-day security operations. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Granting a monitoring service account read-only access to only the specific logs it needs to collect", "Granting every new hire full domain administrator rights by default to avoid future access-request tickets", "Configuring a backup service account to write only to its designated backup repository, with no rights elsewhere", "Sharing one generic automation credential across every scheduled task in the environment"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Scoping a monitoring account to read-only access over just the logs it needs, and scoping a backup account to write only to its own repository, are both textbook examples of granting a non-human account the minimum rights required for its function. Defaulting every new hire to domain admin, and reusing one shared credential across unrelated automated tasks, are the opposite of least privilege - both create broad, unnecessary, and hard-to-trace access.$q$),
('ci-q-1611','cissp',7,'7.4','ordering',2,$q$Order the stages of using a break-glass emergency access account, from the first action taken to the last.$q$,$q$["An outage or emergency meeting the defined criteria for break-glass use is declared", "The vaulted break-glass credential is checked out, immediately triggering an alert to the security team", "The administrator performs the necessary emergency action using the credential", "The credential is returned and its password is automatically rotated", "A mandatory post-use review is conducted to confirm the access was appropriate and necessary"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Break-glass access follows a deliberate order that balances speed against accountability: an emergency must first genuinely meet the declared criteria, the credential's checkout triggers real-time alerting rather than silent use, the emergency action is performed, the credential is immediately rotated so it cannot be reused, and only afterward is a mandatory review conducted to confirm the access was justified - skipping the alerting or the post-use review would turn a controlled emergency mechanism into an unmonitored standing backdoor.$q$),
('ci-q-1612','cissp',7,'7.4','matching',3,$q$Match each foundational operations term on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Dual control (two-person integrity)","Break-glass access","Operational Level Agreement (OLA)","Individual accountability"],"right":["Ensures every privileged action can be traced to the specific individual who performed it, rather than a shared credential","Requires two people to act together, at the same time, to complete one sensitive operational action","An internal, team-to-team commitment that underpins the organization's ability to meet an externally facing SLA","A vaulted emergency credential that triggers alerting and requires mandatory post-use review whenever it is checked out"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,2,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$These four concepts are easy to blur together because they all involve controlling who can do what and when: dual control demands simultaneous joint action on one operation, break-glass access is a tightly monitored exception path for emergencies, an OLA is the internal agreement that makes an external SLA achievable, and individual accountability ties every privileged action to one traceable person rather than a shared identity.$q$),
-- 7.5 apply resource protection (ci-q-1613..1626)
('ci-q-1613','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',1,$q$An organization stores its offsite backup tapes in a locked media library where only a designated media custodian may check tapes in or out, and every checkout is logged with the requester's name, date, and business justification. What resource-protection purpose does restricting and logging tape checkout serve?$q$,$q$["It ensures accountability for who has physical possession of sensitive backup media at any given time and deters unauthorized removal", "It replaces the need to encrypt the backup data itself", "It satisfies the organization's data classification requirements on its own", "It is required only for media containing publicly releasable data"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A media library with a controlling custodian and a checkout log establishes physical accountability for sensitive media - at any moment, the organization knows who has possession and why, and unauthorized removal is both harder to accomplish and easier to detect. It complements, but does not replace, encryption of the data itself or the classification decision that determined the media needed this level of handling in the first place.$q$),
('ci-q-1614','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',2,$q$Backup tapes are transported to an offsite storage facility in tamper-evident, sealed containers via a bonded courier, with a signed custody record obtained at both pickup and delivery. Which risk does this transport procedure primarily mitigate?$q$,$q$["Loss, theft, or undetected tampering with sensitive media while it is outside the organization's direct physical control", "The risk that the backup itself is technically corrupted or unreadable", "The risk that the offsite facility lacks fire suppression", "The need to classify the data before it is backed up"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Media in transit is, by definition, temporarily outside the organization's direct physical oversight, which is exactly when it is most vulnerable to loss, theft, or tampering. Tamper-evident packaging reveals if a container was opened in transit, a bonded courier adds accountability and insurance backing, and signed custody records at each handoff create a documented trail - none of which addresses the technical integrity of the backup data or facility-level environmental controls.$q$),
('ci-q-1615','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',1,$q$A vendor technician arrives on-site to service a server that stores regulated customer data. Facility policy requires that the technician be escorted by internal staff and remain within a monitored area for the entire visit. What resource-protection principle does this escort requirement enforce?$q$,$q$["Maintaining physical oversight of anyone who has hands-on access to sensitive equipment, even authorized, trusted third-party personnel", "Least privilege applied to network access", "Job rotation among internal staff", "Encryption of data at rest"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Being an authorized, trusted vendor does not eliminate the need for physical oversight - an escort ensures that hands-on activity around sensitive equipment is directly observed and can be interrupted or questioned in real time, regardless of how much the organization otherwise trusts the vendor relationship. This is a physical custody control, distinct from network-level access restrictions, personnel rotation, or data-at-rest encryption.$q$),
('ci-q-1616','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',2,$q$A server's failed drive is still under vendor warranty. Before shipping the drive back for a warranty replacement (RMA), the IT team removes it from the return package, performs an approved sanitization pass on it, and keeps a spare drive in its place instead of returning the failed one untouched. Why sanitize a drive being returned to a trusted vendor under warranty?$q$,$q$["Even a legitimate, trusted vendor's repair facility is outside the organization's direct control, and a failed drive may still contain recoverable sensitive data", "Sanitizing voids the warranty and is done specifically to prevent a free replacement", "The vendor legally requires proof of data loss before honoring any warranty claim", "It is unnecessary, since a drive that has failed can never be read again by anyone"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A drive being "failed" from the server's perspective does not mean its data is unrecoverable - specialized forensic techniques can often still extract data from a drive that no longer functions normally in its original system. Because the RMA process hands physical custody of the media to a party outside the organization's control, resource protection calls for sanitizing (or retaining and destroying) the drive rather than returning it as-is, regardless of how trustworthy the vendor is generally assumed to be.$q$),
('ci-q-1617','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',2,$q$A data center keeps a locked, inventoried cabinet of cold-spare hard drives, network cards, and power supplies for rapid hardware replacement, and periodically tests the spares to confirm they still function. What is the security rationale for locking and inventorying spare hardware, beyond simply preventing theft?$q$,$q$["Untracked spares could be swapped in without a record, or a compromised or tampered spare could be substituted without anyone noticing, undermining the integrity of the replacement process", "Spare hardware never needs to be tracked once it has already been purchased", "It is required only when the spares contain firmware that can be updated", "Locking the spares eliminates the need to ever test whether they still function"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Spare hardware is a resource that needs the same custody discipline as production equipment: without inventory control, there is no record of exactly which spare was installed when, and a tampered or compromised spare could be substituted into production without detection. Locking the cabinet controls physical access, but inventory tracking and periodic functional testing are separate, complementary controls that address different failure modes.$q$),
('ci-q-1618','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',1,$q$A technician opens a server chassis to replace a memory module and wears a grounded anti-static wrist strap throughout the procedure. What risk is this precaution primarily intended to prevent?$q$,$q$["Electrostatic discharge (ESD) damaging sensitive electronic components during handling", "Unauthorized access to the server's stored data", "Fire caused by an electrical short inside the chassis", "Theft of the component being replaced"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Electronic components, especially memory and other sensitive circuitry, can be silently damaged by a static discharge from a person's body that they would never even feel. A grounded wrist strap continuously bleeds off any static charge before it can arc into the component, protecting hardware integrity during maintenance - a distinct concern from data confidentiality, fire safety, or theft prevention.$q$),
('ci-q-1619','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',2,$q$Equipment retired from production is not destroyed immediately; instead it is placed in a locked, access-controlled holding area, still logged as a tracked asset, until a scheduled destruction event occurs. Why maintain physical security controls over decommissioned equipment that is already scheduled for destruction?$q$,$q$["Retired equipment can still contain live, recoverable sensitive data until it is actually sanitized or destroyed, so it remains a protection-worthy asset in the interim", "Because destruction of equipment is illegal without a court order", "Because decommissioned equipment automatically loses its classification level the moment it is retired", "Because the holding area itself substitutes for actually performing the destruction"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Being scheduled for destruction does not make equipment safe to leave unsecured in the meantime - until sanitization or destruction actually happens, the drives and components can still hold live sensitive data, so the equipment needs the same physical protection as any other asset carrying that data. A holding area is a control that bridges the gap between decommissioning and disposition; it is not a substitute for actually completing the destruction step.$q$),
('ci-q-1620','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',3,$q$An organization contracts a third-party vendor to physically destroy retired hard drives. To have real assurance that destruction actually occurred as required, rather than simply trusting the vendor's invoice, what should the organization obtain and verify?$q$,$q$["A certificate of destruction documenting serial numbers and method, ideally from a vendor holding recognized media-destruction certification such as NAID AAA, with the option to witness or audit the process", "A verbal confirmation from the vendor's sales representative", "Nothing further is needed once a destruction contract has been signed", "A generic recycling receipt with no serial numbers or destruction method listed"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A signed contract only establishes an obligation; it does not by itself prove the obligation was fulfilled. A proper certificate of destruction ties specific serial numbers to a specific method and date, and independent certification such as NAID AAA (along with the option to witness or audit the process) gives the organization real, checkable assurance that the media was actually destroyed as required, rather than relying on trust alone.$q$),
('ci-q-1621','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',2,$q$A development team wants to use realistic data to test a new feature. Instead of copying live production customer records onto test servers, the team is required to use masked or synthetically generated data that mimics production characteristics without containing any real customer information. What resource-protection goal does this practice serve?$q$,$q$["It limits the exposure of sensitive production data to lower-security test and development hardware and environments", "It guarantees the test results will exactly match production behavior in every case", "It removes the need for any access controls on the test environment", "It is required only when the test environment happens to be hosted in the cloud"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Test and development environments are typically held to a lower security bar than production - fewer monitoring controls, broader developer access, weaker change control - so copying live sensitive data onto them multiplies the number of places that data is exposed to compromise. Masked or synthetic data lets testing remain realistic enough to be useful while removing the actual sensitive content from an environment less equipped to protect it.$q$),
('ci-q-1622','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',1,$q$Before an employee is allowed to take a company laptop home or on travel, the IT asset system requires the laptop to be formally checked out against the employee's name, and the employee must acknowledge handling requirements. What does this check-out process primarily protect against?$q$,$q$["Loss of accountability for who has physical possession of a portable, easily lost or stolen asset outside the office", "The laptop's manufacturer warranty expiring early", "The need for the laptop to have antivirus software installed", "The employee's network account being automatically disabled"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A formal check-out record ties a specific portable asset to a specific person at a specific time, which matters precisely because portable equipment leaving controlled facility space is far more exposed to loss or theft than equipment that stays put. Without that record, there is no reliable way to know who had the device if it goes missing, or to hold anyone accountable for following the acknowledged handling requirements.$q$),
('ci-q-1623','cissp',7,'7.5','multi',1,$q$Select the TWO practices that best protect portable and removable media, such as USB drives, backup tapes, and external disks, as part of day-to-day resource protection. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Encrypting sensitive data stored on removable media so its loss does not automatically expose the contents", "Allowing any employee to check removable media in or out of storage without logging who took it", "Storing removable media in a locked, access-controlled location when it is not in active use", "Leaving backup tapes on an unattended desk overnight before offsite courier pickup"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$Encrypting removable media means that a lost or stolen device does not automatically translate into a data exposure, and storing media in a locked, access-controlled location when idle limits opportunistic theft or tampering. Allowing untracked, unlogged access and leaving media unattended in the open do the opposite - both directly increase the chance of undetected loss or compromise.$q$),
('ci-q-1624','cissp',7,'7.5','ordering',2,$q$Order the stages of a piece of hardware's lifecycle from a resource-protection standpoint, from the first stage to the last.$q$,$q$["The asset is procured and registered in the inventory system with a unique tag", "The asset is deployed into production and its location and owner are tracked", "Maintenance or repair events on the asset are logged and performed under supervision", "The asset is decommissioned and moved to a secured holding area pending disposition", "The asset is sanitized or destroyed, a certificate is obtained, and it is removed from inventory"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Resource protection follows the asset from birth to disposal: registration establishes accountability from day one, tracked deployment keeps that accountability current, supervised maintenance events prevent gaps in oversight while the asset is in active use, a secured holding area protects it during the vulnerable window between decommissioning and destruction, and final sanitization or destruction with documented proof closes the lifecycle cleanly.$q$),
('ci-q-1625','cissp',7,'7.5','matching',2,$q$Match each resource-protection term on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Media librarian","RMA (return merchandise authorization)","ESD wrist strap","Certificate of destruction"],"right":["A grounding tool that prevents static electricity from damaging sensitive electronic components during handling","Documented proof, ideally from a certified vendor, that specific media was destroyed as required","The custodian responsible for controlling check-in and check-out of physical media in a locked library","The process of returning failed hardware to a vendor for warranty repair or replacement"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,3,0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$Each of these plays a distinct role across resource protection: a media librarian governs custody of physical media day to day, RMA governs how failed hardware safely leaves the organization's control for repair, an ESD wrist strap protects component integrity during hands-on maintenance, and a certificate of destruction provides verifiable proof at the end of an asset's life - together spanning custody, maintenance, and disposal.$q$),
('ci-q-1626','cissp',7,'7.5','mcq',3,$q$A hospital sends a batch of retired imaging workstations to a recycler without removing their drives, without obtaining any documentation from the recycler, and without recording the removal in the asset inventory. Which of the following best describes the resource-protection failure in this scenario?$q$,$q$["A combined failure to sanitize or destroy the media, verify destruction with documentation, and maintain accurate asset inventory records", "A failure of change management, and nothing else", "A failure of network segmentation, and nothing else", "No failure occurred, since recyclers are inherently trustworthy by default"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$This scenario stacks several resource-protection failures at once: sensitive data-bearing drives left the organization's control without any sanitization or destruction, no certificate or documentation was obtained to verify what actually happened to them, and the asset inventory was never updated to reflect the disposal - none of which has anything to do with change management or network segmentation, and none of which is excused simply because a recycler is generally assumed to be reputable.$q$),
-- 7.6 incident management phases (ci-q-1627..1639)
('ci-q-1627','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',1,$q$A SIEM alert fires indicating unusual outbound traffic from a finance server. Before treating this as an active incident and activating the full response process, an analyst first confirms the traffic pattern is genuinely anomalous and not an authorized backup job or benign application update. What incident management phase does this initial validation belong to?$q$,$q$["Detection - confirming a security event is a genuine incident worth escalating before further action is taken", "Recovery - restoring the server to normal operation", "Remediation - fixing whatever caused the underlying issue", "Lessons learned - reviewing what happened after the incident is closed"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Detection is the phase where a security event is first identified and validated as a real incident, rather than a false positive or benign activity, before anything else happens. Everything that follows - mobilizing the team, containing the impact, restoring service - depends on this initial confirmation being made correctly.$q$),
('ci-q-1628','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',1,$q$Once an incident is confirmed, the on-call analyst formally activates the incident response plan, pages the incident response team, and opens a tracked incident case. Which incident management phase does this activation activity represent?$q$,$q$["Response - formally mobilizing the incident response process and team once an incident has been confirmed", "Detection - identifying that something anomalous occurred", "Recovery - returning affected systems to normal service", "Reporting - notifying external regulators of the incident"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Response is the phase where the organization formally mobilizes: activating the incident response plan, assembling the team, and opening the case that will track the incident through its lifecycle. It follows detection (which only confirms something real happened) and precedes the active containment work of mitigation.$q$),
('ci-q-1629','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',2,$q$During an active ransomware incident, the response team isolates the affected VLAN from the rest of the network and blocks the attacker's known command-and-control domains at the firewall, specifically to stop the incident from spreading further while investigation continues. Which incident management phase does this action belong to?$q$,$q$["Mitigation - actively limiting the scope and impact of an ongoing incident", "Reporting - notifying stakeholders of the incident's existence", "Lessons learned - documenting what went wrong afterward", "Detection - identifying the initial alert that started the response"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Mitigation covers the actions taken while an incident is still active to limit how far it spreads and how much damage it does - isolating a compromised network segment and blocking known malicious infrastructure are classic mitigation moves. It is distinct from response (mobilizing the team) and from recovery (restoring normal operation afterward), even though all three phases can feel like they blur together under pressure.$q$),
('ci-q-1630','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',2,$q$After confirming that a breach exposed regulated personal data, the organization notifies its executive leadership and legal counsel internally, and, within the legally required timeframe, notifies the affected individuals and the relevant regulator externally. Which incident management phase encompasses these internal and external notifications?$q$,$q$["Reporting - communicating incident details to the appropriate internal and external stakeholders, including any legally mandated notifications", "Mitigation - containing the spread of the incident", "Recovery - restoring affected systems to normal operation", "Detection - identifying that a breach had occurred"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Reporting covers all of the required communication about an incident - both internal escalation to leadership and legal, and any externally mandated notification to regulators or affected individuals under applicable breach-notification law. It is a distinct phase from the technical work of containing the incident or restoring systems, and missing a legally mandated notification deadline is itself a compliance failure on top of the original incident.$q$),
('ci-q-1631','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',1,$q$Before reconnecting a previously compromised server back to the production network, the response team first verifies from clean backups or a rebuilt image that no attacker tooling or backdoor remains, and only then restores normal service. What incident management phase does restoring the verified-clean system to normal operation represent?$q$,$q$["Recovery - returning systems to normal, trusted operation after an incident", "Mitigation - limiting the spread of an ongoing incident", "Response - the initial mobilization of the incident response team", "Detection - confirming that an alert represents a real incident"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Recovery is the phase focused on getting affected systems back into normal, trusted service - but doing so safely requires first confirming the system is actually clean, since reconnecting a still-compromised host would simply reintroduce the same problem. This is a later-stage activity than mitigation, which is about containing an incident that is still actively unfolding.$q$),
('ci-q-1632','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',3,$q$After systems are back online and operating normally following an incident caused by an unpatched vulnerability, the team still schedules and applies the missing patch across every affected system, and revises the vulnerability-scanning process so the gap cannot recur unnoticed. Which incident management phase does this root-cause fix represent, and how does it differ from the phase that came before it?$q$,$q$["Remediation - addressing the underlying cause so the same incident cannot recur, distinct from recovery, which restores normal operation without necessarily fixing what allowed the incident to happen in the first place", "Recovery - this is simply the same activity as restoring service, performed a little later", "Detection - identifying that a vulnerability existed in the first place", "Reporting - notifying regulators of the underlying root cause"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Recovery and remediation are easy to conflate but answer different questions: recovery asks "are the systems back to normal operation?" while remediation asks "have we fixed what actually let this happen so it cannot recur?" A system can be fully recovered - up, running, serving users - while the underlying vulnerability that caused the incident is still unpatched, which is exactly why remediation is treated as its own distinct, later phase rather than folded into recovery.$q$),
('ci-q-1633','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',2,$q$Two weeks after an incident is closed, the response team holds a structured review meeting to identify what worked, what did not, and updates the incident response playbook based on the findings. Which incident management phase does this post-incident review represent?$q$,$q$["Lessons learned - reviewing the incident after closure to improve future response", "Mitigation - limiting the impact of the incident while it was still active", "Reporting - the mandatory notification step performed during the incident", "Detection - the initial identification of the incident"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Lessons learned is the final phase, conducted after the incident is fully closed, where the team deliberately steps back to evaluate the response itself: what worked, what fell short, and what should change in the playbook, tooling, or training as a result. Skipping this phase means the organization is likely to repeat the same avoidable mistakes in its next incident.$q$),
('ci-q-1634','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',1,$q$An organization defines four incident severity levels, where a Severity 1 incident, such as active data exfiltration from a production database, triggers immediate executive notification and full incident response team activation, while a Severity 4 incident, such as a single blocked phishing email, is simply logged for trend analysis without escalation. What is the purpose of tiered incident severity classification?$q$,$q$["It ensures response urgency and resources scale appropriately with actual business impact, rather than treating every incident identically", "It replaces the need for a documented incident response plan altogether", "It is required only for incidents that involve an external attacker", "It determines which vendor support contract legally applies to the incident"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Not every security event deserves the same response intensity - treating a single blocked phishing email the same as active data exfiltration would either waste resources on trivial events or, worse, under-react to genuinely severe ones. Severity tiers give the organization a consistent, pre-agreed way to match response urgency and escalation level to actual business impact.$q$),
('ci-q-1635','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',1,$q$During a major incident, one designated person is responsible for coordinating the overall response and making key decisions, and for ensuring different workstreams, such as technical investigation, legal, and communications, stay aligned - without necessarily performing hands-on technical work themselves. What role is this?$q$,$q$["Incident commander - coordinates the overall response and decision-making across all workstreams", "Forensic analyst - performs hands-on evidence collection", "Media relations contact only", "Data owner - approves changes to a data asset's classification"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The incident commander role exists specifically to provide a single point of coordination and decision-making authority during a chaotic, fast-moving incident, keeping the technical, legal, and communications workstreams aligned rather than working at cross purposes. This coordination function is distinct from any single hands-on technical role, such as a forensic analyst gathering evidence.$q$),
('ci-q-1636','cissp',7,'7.6','mcq',2,$q$Rather than improvising a response from scratch every time, a security team maintains predefined, step-by-step playbooks for common incident types, such as ransomware, business email compromise, and denial-of-service, specifying the expected actions and decision points for each. What is the primary benefit of using predefined playbooks during incident response?$q$,$q$["They speed up and standardize response by providing tested, repeatable steps for known incident patterns, reducing reliance on improvisation under pressure", "They eliminate the need for an incident commander on any incident", "They guarantee that the same type of incident can never recur again", "They are required only for incidents that involve regulated personal data"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A playbook captures the accumulated, tested knowledge of how to respond to a known incident pattern, so the team is not reinventing the response under the stress and time pressure of a live event. It speeds up and standardizes response, but it does not remove the need for coordinated decision-making, and it does nothing on its own to prevent the same category of incident from happening again - that is remediation's job.$q$),
('ci-q-1637','cissp',7,'7.6','multi',2,$q$When attacker activity is discovered on a compromised host, the response team must choose a containment strategy. Select the TWO considerations that legitimately factor into deciding whether to immediately isolate the host versus quietly monitor the attacker for a period to gather intelligence. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["Whether the ongoing risk of letting the attacker remain active, such as further data loss or lateral movement, outweighs the intelligence value of observing them", "The attacker's astrological sign", "Whether monitoring can be done safely without risking further compromise while intelligence is gathered", "The brand of antivirus software installed on completely unrelated systems"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$The isolate-versus-monitor decision is a genuine risk trade-off: immediate isolation stops further damage but ends the chance to observe the attacker's tools and objectives, while monitoring can yield valuable intelligence but only if it can be done without letting the attacker do more harm in the meantime. Irrelevant factors like an attacker's astrology or unrelated systems' antivirus brand play no role in this decision.$q$),
('ci-q-1638','cissp',7,'7.6','ordering',2,$q$Order the incident management phases as defined in the CISSP CBK, from first to last.$q$,$q$["Detection","Response","Mitigation","Reporting","Recovery","Remediation","Lessons learned"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]$q$::jsonb,$q$The seven phases flow in a logical sequence: an incident is first detected and confirmed, the response process is mobilized, mitigation actively limits the damage while it is unfolding, reporting communicates the incident to the right internal and external parties, recovery restores normal operation, remediation fixes the underlying cause so it cannot recur, and lessons learned closes the loop by improving the process for next time.$q$),
('ci-q-1639','cissp',7,'7.6','matching',3,$q$Match each incident management phase on the left to the action that most distinguishes it on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Mitigation","Remediation","Recovery","Reporting"],"right":["Returns affected systems to normal, trusted operation","Stops the incident from getting worse right now, while it is still active","Communicates incident facts to the people who legally or organizationally need to know","Fixes the underlying cause so the same incident cannot happen again"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[1,3,0,2]$q$::jsonb,$q$These four phases are frequently confused because they can all be happening in the same general timeframe, but each answers a distinct question: mitigation asks how to stop things from getting worse right now, reporting asks who needs to be told and when, recovery asks how to get back to normal, and remediation asks how to make sure it cannot happen again - understanding which question each phase answers is what keeps them from blurring together on the exam.$q$),
-- 7.7 operate and maintain detective and preventative measures (ci-q-1640..1653)
('ci-q-1640','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',1,$q$An endpoint protection platform (EPP) blocks a never-before-seen piece of malware not because it matches any known malware signature, but because the program's runtime behavior, rapidly encrypting many files and calling out to an unfamiliar external host, matches a suspicious activity pattern. What detection approach caught this malware?$q$,$q$["Behavioral (heuristic-based) detection, which flags suspicious activity patterns rather than relying on matching known signatures", "Signature-based detection, matching the file's hash against a known-malware database", "Application allowlisting, which blocks anything not explicitly approved", "Perimeter network firewall filtering"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Because the malware had never been seen before, it could not have matched any existing signature - what caught it was its behavior at runtime (mass file encryption plus suspicious outbound communication), which is the defining trait of behavioral or heuristic detection. This complements, rather than replaces, signature-based detection, which is fast and reliable for already-known threats but blind to genuinely new ones.$q$),
('ci-q-1641','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',2,$q$Beyond simply blocking known-bad files like traditional antivirus, an Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) tool continuously records process, file, and network activity on each endpoint, letting an analyst later search that history to trace exactly how an attacker moved between processes, and lets the analyst remotely isolate the endpoint from the network. What capability does this describe that goes beyond conventional antivirus?$q$,$q$["Continuous endpoint telemetry recording plus investigation and remote response capability, not just point-in-time blocking of known-bad files", "A firewall's stateful packet inspection", "A vulnerability scanner's periodic scanning schedule", "A backup solution's file versioning history"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Traditional antivirus is fundamentally a blocking tool: it compares files against known-bad signatures and stops matches. EDR adds an ongoing recording and investigation layer on top - a searchable history of endpoint activity that lets an analyst reconstruct an attack after the fact, plus the ability to actively respond, such as remotely isolating a compromised host - capabilities that have nothing to do with firewalls, scanners, or backup tools.$q$),
('ci-q-1642','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',1,$q$Rather than maintaining a list of known-bad executables to block, an organization configures its endpoints so that only explicitly approved, digitally signed applications are permitted to run at all - anything not on the approved list is denied by default. What is this execution-control approach called?$q$,$q$["Application allowlisting (default-deny execution control)", "Application blocklisting", "Network access control (NAC)", "Data loss prevention (DLP)"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Allowlisting flips the default from "permit unless known bad" to "deny unless explicitly approved," so it can stop malware it has never seen before simply because that malware is not on the approved list - a fundamentally different posture from network access control, which governs whether a device can join the network, or DLP, which governs data leaving the organization.$q$),
('ci-q-1643','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',2,$q$A security team debates whether to use application allowlisting or application blocklisting on a fleet of tightly-managed kiosk terminals that only ever run three known applications. Which factor makes allowlisting a stronger fit than blocklisting for this specific environment?$q$,$q$["Because the set of legitimate applications is small and well-known, allowlisting can deny everything else by default, including malware never seen before - something a blocklist, which only blocks what it already knows about, cannot do", "Blocklisting is always more secure than allowlisting in absolutely every environment", "Allowlisting requires no ongoing maintenance once it is first configured", "Kiosk terminals are technically incapable of running either type of execution control"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Allowlisting works best precisely when the legitimate set of applications is small, stable, and well-known, because then defining "everything else is denied" is manageable and catches unknown threats by default. On a general-purpose workstation where users need to run arbitrary new software constantly, that same default-deny posture becomes much more operationally burdensome to maintain - which is why the fit depends heavily on the environment, not a blanket rule that one approach always beats the other.$q$),
('ci-q-1644','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',2,$q$Before delivering an email attachment to a user's inbox, a security gateway first opens the attachment inside an isolated, instrumented virtual environment and observes its behavior for a short period, only releasing the attachment to the user if it shows no malicious activity. What is this technique called?$q$,$q$["Sandboxing (dynamic detonation analysis) of the attachment before delivery", "Application allowlisting", "Signature-based antivirus scanning", "Network access control posture checking"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Sandboxing detonates a suspicious file in an isolated, monitored environment and observes what it actually does at runtime before it ever reaches a real user, which is especially valuable against malware crafted to evade static signature matching. It is a dynamic, behavior-based analysis technique, distinct from allowlisting (a static, pre-approved list) or posture checking (evaluating a device's compliance state).$q$),
('ci-q-1645','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',3,$q$A security team plants a fake, monitored database record containing a bogus but realistic-looking customer account and API credential inside a production database. Any attempt to actually use that fake credential immediately triggers an alert, since no legitimate process is ever supposed to touch it. What is this decoy called, and how does it differ from a honeypot?$q$,$q$["A honeytoken - a decoy piece of DATA (or credential) rather than an entire decoy SYSTEM, planted inside real production systems to trigger an alert on unauthorized use", "A honeynet - a network made up of multiple decoy systems", "A DMZ record that serves no security purpose", "A canary system, which is simply another name for a honeypot"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A honeypot is an entire decoy system built to attract and study attacker activity in isolation from production. A honeytoken instead embeds a single fake, monitored piece of data or credential directly inside a real production system - since no legitimate process should ever reference it, any use of it is a near-certain sign of unauthorized access, without needing to stand up an entire separate decoy environment.$q$),
('ci-q-1646','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',2,$q$A security team deploys a honeypot that passively appears vulnerable and simply waits to see who attacks it, without the organization ever advertising it or actively encouraging anyone to attack it. From a legal standpoint, why does this passive deployment approach matter?$q$,$q$["Passively waiting to be attacked (enticement) is generally legally defensible, whereas actively luring or encouraging someone who would not otherwise have attacked (entrapment-like inducement) raises legal and evidentiary risk", "Honeypots are illegal in every jurisdiction regardless of how they are deployed", "Legal risk from a honeypot only exists if the honeypot is physically located outside the country", "Passive deployment removes any requirement to isolate the honeypot from production systems"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The legal distinction between enticement and entrapment matters directly for how a honeypot is deployed operationally: a decoy that simply sits and waits to see who chooses to attack it (enticement) is on much firmer legal ground than one that actively lures or induces an attack that would not otherwise have happened, which can undermine both the legality of the deployment and the usability of any resulting evidence.$q$),
('ci-q-1647','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',1,$q$An intrusion prevention system's effectiveness against newly emerging attack techniques depends heavily on its signature database and threat-intelligence feeds being kept current. What operational practice most directly ensures the IPS keeps detecting new threats over time, rather than only the threats known when it was first deployed?$q$,$q$["Regularly updating signatures and integrating current threat-intelligence feeds into the IPS", "Leaving the IPS's initial configuration permanently unchanged after deployment", "Disabling the IPS entirely during scheduled maintenance windows", "Relying solely on the IPS's original factory-default ruleset indefinitely"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$A detection or prevention tool is only as current as the intelligence feeding it - without regular signature updates and fresh threat-intelligence feeds, an IPS effectively freezes at the threat landscape that existed on the day it was configured, becoming steadily less useful as new attack techniques emerge. Operating and maintaining these tools is an ongoing responsibility, not a one-time deployment task.$q$),
('ci-q-1648','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',3,$q$During a quarterly firewall audit, a security team discovers dozens of rules referencing decommissioned servers and one overly broad "allow any-any" rule left over from a troubleshooting session years earlier that was never removed. What operational practice is designed to catch and remove exactly this kind of rule-base decay over time?$q$,$q$["Periodic firewall rule-base review and audit, removing stale, overly permissive, or undocumented rules", "One-time firewall configuration performed only at initial deployment and never revisited", "Network segmentation applied elsewhere in the environment", "Intrusion prevention signature tuning"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Firewall rule bases accumulate cruft over time as systems are decommissioned, projects end, and temporary troubleshooting rules never get cleaned up - each stale or overly permissive rule is a small, forgotten expansion of the attack surface. A periodic review and audit process exists specifically to catch and remove exactly this kind of drift, which a one-time initial configuration can never account for on its own.$q$),
('ci-q-1649','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',1,$q$When an endpoint protection platform confirms a file as malicious on one laptop, it automatically distributes a block for that file's hash to every other managed endpoint and quarantines the laptop from the network pending investigation. What operational benefit does this fleet-wide automatic response provide?$q$,$q$["It limits the malware's ability to spread to or affect other endpoints before a human analyst can even respond, shrinking the window of exposure", "It permanently deletes the endpoint protection platform's own historical logs", "It replaces the need for any human review of the incident afterward", "It only functions correctly if the detected malware turns out to be a false positive"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Automated, fleet-wide response closes the gap between detection on one machine and protection across the whole environment - instead of waiting for a human analyst to manually push the same block everywhere, the tool immediately propagates it, shrinking the window during which other endpoints remain exposed to the same threat. It supports human investigation rather than replacing it.$q$),
('ci-q-1650','cissp',7,'7.7','multi',1,$q$Select the TWO accurate statements about YARA rules as used in anti-malware and threat-hunting operations. (Choose two.)$q$,$q$["YARA rules describe patterns, such as strings, byte sequences, or characteristics, used to identify and classify files as matching a known malware family", "YARA rules can be written and shared by analysts to hunt for a specific malware family across a fleet of endpoints or a set of files", "YARA rules can only be used to configure network firewall access control lists", "YARA is a proprietary tool that cannot be customized or extended by security analysts"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1]$q$::jsonb,$q$YARA is a widely used, freely customizable pattern-matching tool for describing and detecting malware families by their characteristics, and analysts routinely author and share YARA rules to hunt for a specific threat across many systems or files at once. It has nothing to do with configuring firewall access control lists, and it is neither proprietary nor closed to customization.$q$),
('ci-q-1651','cissp',7,'7.7','ordering',2,$q$Order the stages of a sandboxed email attachment workflow, from the first step to the last.$q$,$q$["An email carrying an attachment arrives at the security gateway", "The attachment is redirected to an isolated sandbox environment", "The sandbox executes (detonates) the file and observes its runtime behavior", "A verdict of benign or malicious is determined based on the observed behavior", "The attachment is released to the recipient's inbox or blocked and quarantined based on the verdict"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$The workflow deliberately delays delivery until analysis is complete: the message arrives, its attachment is diverted into isolation rather than being delivered immediately, the sandbox actually runs the file to observe what it does, a verdict is reached from that observed behavior, and only then is the final delivery-or-block decision made - reordering any of these steps, such as delivering before detonation, would defeat the entire purpose.$q$),
('ci-q-1652','cissp',7,'7.7','matching',2,$q$Match each detective/preventative technique on the left to its correct description on the right.$q$,$q${"left":["Allowlisting","Blocklisting","Sandboxing","Honeytoken"],"right":["A decoy piece of data or credential planted to detect unauthorized use","Blocks only items matching a known-bad list, permitting everything else by default","Denies everything by default except an explicitly approved set of applications","Executes a suspicious file in an isolated environment to observe its behavior before it reaches a user"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[2,1,3,0]$q$::jsonb,$q$These four techniques operate on different logic: allowlisting and blocklisting are opposite default postures for deciding what may execute, sandboxing is a dynamic behavioral check performed before delivery rather than a static list at all, and a honeytoken is a detective decoy rather than a preventative control - distinguishing preventative from detective purpose, and static from dynamic analysis, is exactly what this question tests.$q$),
('ci-q-1653','cissp',7,'7.7','mcq',3,$q$A security architect wants to both block known malware efficiently and also catch a brand-new, custom-built piece of malware that no vendor has ever seen before, without relying on a single point of failure. Which combination of controls best achieves both goals together?$q$,$q$["Signature-based anti-malware for known threats, combined with behavioral/EDR-based detection and sandboxing to catch novel, unsigned malware based on what it does rather than what it matches", "Signature-based anti-malware alone, since it is sufficient for detecting malware of any age", "Application blocklisting alone, since a blocklist can anticipate all malware that will ever be written", "A single honeypot deployed at the network perimeter, since it alone detects every type of malware"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$No single detective or preventative technique covers every case: signature-based tools are fast and reliable against known threats but blind to anything genuinely new, while behavioral detection, EDR, and sandboxing catch novel malware precisely because they judge it by what it does rather than whether it matches something already catalogued. Layering both, rather than depending on one alone, is what avoids a single point of failure.$q$);

insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- 7.4 core (ci-f-1600..1609)
('ci-f-1600','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$Why is unannounced, randomly-timed job rotation more effective at detecting fraud than a rotation schedule published a year in advance?$$,$$A known future rotation date gives someone concealing fraud or errors time to prepare and hide evidence before it happens. Removing that advance warning means whoever steps into the role next is far more likely to stumble onto irregularities before they can be covered up.$$),
('ci-f-1601','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$Why should a privileged administrator's system access be suspended during their mandatory vacation, not just their physical presence removed?$$,$$If the access stays active, the administrator can log in remotely during "vacation" and keep concealing whatever they were hiding, which defeats the entire fraud-detection purpose of the policy. Someone else must actually be forced to operate in their place for irregularities to have a chance to surface.$$),
('ci-f-1602','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$How does dual control (two-person integrity) apply to an operational action like a production database restore, as opposed to the classic vault-key example?$$,$$The mechanism is identical even though the scenario differs: two authorized people must independently authenticate and approve the destructive restore command at the same time, and neither person's approval alone is sufficient to execute it - the same simultaneous-joint-action requirement, just applied to an IT operations task instead of a physical vault.$$),
('ci-f-1603','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$What does "least privilege applied operationally" mean when the subject is a CI/CD pipeline's service account rather than a human user?$$,$$It means scoping the service account down to only the specific resource types and actions each pipeline stage actually needs, rather than granting broad standing administrative rights across every environment for convenience. An overprivileged automation credential is a common real-world risk, since a single compromise can affect everything that credential can touch.$$),
('ci-f-1604','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$What is a break-glass (emergency access) account, and what controls typically surround it?$$,$$A vaulted, rarely-used credential reserved for genuine emergencies, checked out only when defined criteria are met. It is compensated for by real-time alerting to the security team the moment it is checked out, automatic credential rotation after use, and a mandatory post-use review confirming the access was appropriate.$$),
('ci-f-1605','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$How does an on-call rotation differ in purpose from job rotation used as a fraud-detection control?$$,$$On-call rotation exists to distribute after-hours workload and ensure coverage without burning out any one person. Job-rotation-as-fraud-control instead deliberately places a different person into a role to expose concealed wrongdoing. They can look similar (people taking turns) while serving entirely different purposes.$$),
('ci-f-1606','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$Why should the person who reviews a firewall administrator's change logs be someone other than the administrator themselves?$$,$$Separating the ability to make a change from the ability to independently audit that change means a single person cannot both make an unauthorized modification and be the one checking whether anything suspicious happened - a form of separation of duties applied to log review specifically.$$),
('ci-f-1607','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$What compensating controls help offset the risk when a small team cannot fully separate the requester, approver, and implementer roles in a change process?$$,$$Increased management review of change logs, periodic rotation of who performs each stage among the available staff, and independent audit of completed changes - none of which achieves full separation of duties, but together they raise the odds that abuse or error gets noticed.$$),
('ci-f-1608','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$Why is it a security problem for multiple administrators to share one generic privileged account and password?$$,$$It destroys individual accountability - if a suspicious action is later discovered, there is no way to determine which of the account's users actually performed it, since audit logs can only show the shared identity, not the specific person behind it that day.$$),
('ci-f-1609','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$What is the difference between an external Service Level Agreement (SLA) and an internal Operational Level Agreement (OLA)?$$,$$An SLA is the outward-facing performance commitment made to a customer or partner. An OLA is the internal, team-to-team agreement that underpins the organization's ability to actually meet that external promise - for example, an internal ten-minute escalation commitment that makes a customer-facing one-hour acknowledgment SLA achievable.$$),
-- 7.4 acronym (ci-a-520..523)
('ci-a-520','cissp','acronym',7,'7.4',$$TPI$$,$$Two-Person Integrity - requires two authorized individuals to be present and jointly act to complete one sensitive operation, so no single person can carry it out alone.$$),
('ci-a-521','cissp','acronym',7,'7.4',$$OLA$$,$$Operational Level Agreement - an internal, team-to-team performance commitment that underpins an organization's ability to meet an externally facing SLA.$$),
('ci-a-522','cissp','acronym',7,'7.4',$$PoLP$$,$$Principle of Least Privilege - grants a subject, whether a human user or a service/automation account, only the minimum access rights needed to perform its function and nothing more.$$),
('ci-a-523','cissp','acronym',7,'7.4',$$NTK$$,$$Need to Know - restricts access to specific information to only those whose current duties actually require it, even if they already hold sufficient clearance or role-based rights to see it.$$),
-- 7.4 feynman (ci-y-330..331)
('ci-y-330','cissp','feynman',7,'7.4',$$Explain why a break-glass emergency access account needs MORE monitoring than a normal privileged account, even though it is used far less often.$$,$$Model answer: It seems backwards at first - shouldn't the account you rarely touch need less attention than the one used every day? But think about what break-glass access actually grants: broad, powerful, standing capability, deliberately kept dormant so it's ready the instant a real emergency hits. Its rarity is exactly what makes any use of it so significant - a normal admin account being used on a Tuesday afternoon is unremarkable background noise, but a break-glass credential being checked out is, almost by definition, either a genuine emergency or something going wrong. Because there's no routine pattern of "normal" use to compare against, every single use is an outlier that deserves scrutiny - which is why it gets real-time alerting the moment it's checked out and a mandatory review afterward, rather than just being logged quietly like routine admin activity. The infrequency isn't a reason to relax monitoring; it's the reason every use stands out enough to demand it.$$),
('ci-y-331','cissp','feynman',7,'7.4',$$Explain, with an example, why least privilege applied to a service account is just as important as least privilege applied to a human user.$$,$$Model answer: It's tempting to think of least privilege as a people problem - don't give Bob more access than Bob's job requires - and forget that automated accounts need the exact same discipline, arguably more so. Take a CI/CD deployment service account with standing administrator rights across every cloud subscription, granted "just to be safe" so deployments never fail from a missing permission. That account runs constantly, often triggered by code changes that many developers can influence, and it has no judgment of its own - it will execute whatever the pipeline tells it to, at whatever scope it's been granted. If an attacker manages to inject malicious code into that pipeline, or steal the service account's credential, they inherit every single permission that account holds, instantly, with no human decision-making in the loop to notice something's wrong. A human with excess privilege at least has judgment that might catch a bad instruction; an overprivileged automation account has none. Scoping it down to exactly what each pipeline stage needs means a compromise of that credential is bounded by that narrow scope, not by "everything, everywhere."$$),
-- 7.5 core (ci-f-1610..1618)
('ci-f-1610','cissp','core',7,'7.5',$$What does a media library / media custodian control, and why does it log every checkout?$$,$$A locked media library restricts physical access to sensitive backup media to a designated custodian, and logging every checkout with requester, date, and justification establishes accountability for who has possession of the media at any given moment, deterring unauthorized removal.$$),
('ci-f-1611','cissp','core',7,'7.5',$$What three elements make offsite backup media transport secure, and what risk does each address?$$,$$Tamper-evident sealed packaging reveals if a container was opened in transit; a bonded, accountable courier reduces the chance of casual theft or mishandling; and a signed custody record at pickup and delivery documents who had the media at each handoff, together protecting media during the window it is outside the organization's direct physical control.$$),
('ci-f-1612','cissp','core',7,'7.5',$$Why does policy require escorting even an authorized, trusted vendor technician who is servicing sensitive equipment on-site?$$,$$Trust in the vendor relationship does not eliminate the value of direct physical oversight of hands-on activity around sensitive equipment - an escort ensures the work can be observed and interrupted in real time, regardless of how well-vetted the vendor is generally assumed to be.$$),
('ci-f-1613','cissp','core',7,'7.5',$$Why sanitize a failed drive before returning it to the manufacturer under warranty (RMA), even though the vendor is a trusted party?$$,$$A "failed" drive is not necessarily unreadable - forensic techniques can often still recover data from it - and returning it under RMA hands physical custody to a party outside the organization's control. Sanitizing (or destroying and replacing) the drive removes that exposure regardless of how trustworthy the vendor is assumed to be.$$),
('ci-f-1614','cissp','core',7,'7.5',$$Why are cold-spare hardware components (drives, network cards, power supplies) kept locked and inventoried, not just stored?$$,$$Without inventory control, there is no record of exactly which spare was installed when, and a tampered or compromised spare could be substituted into production without anyone noticing - the lock controls access, while inventory tracking and periodic testing verify the spares are both accounted for and actually functional.$$),
('ci-f-1615','cissp','core',7,'7.5',$$What does an ESD wrist strap protect against during hardware maintenance, and why is the risk easy to overlook?$$,$$It protects sensitive electronic components from electrostatic discharge damage during handling - a static charge far too small for a person to feel can still silently damage circuitry, which is exactly why the risk is easy to dismiss without a grounding precaution in place.$$),
('ci-f-1616','cissp','core',7,'7.5',$$Why does decommissioned equipment still need physical security controls even though it is already scheduled for destruction?$$,$$Being scheduled for destruction does not mean the equipment is safe in the meantime - until sanitization or destruction actually occurs, retired drives and components can still hold live, recoverable sensitive data, so they remain protection-worthy assets during that gap.$$),
('ci-f-1617','cissp','core',7,'7.5',$$What should an organization obtain to have real assurance that a third-party vendor actually destroyed media as contracted, rather than just trusting an invoice?$$,$$A certificate of destruction that ties specific serial numbers to a specific method and date, ideally from a vendor holding recognized independent certification such as NAID AAA, with the option to witness or audit the destruction process directly.$$),
('ci-f-1618','cissp','core',7,'7.5',$$Why do development teams use masked or synthetic data instead of live production data on test/dev hardware?$$,$$Test and development environments are typically held to a lower security bar than production, with fewer monitoring controls and broader developer access, so putting real sensitive data there multiplies the number of weaker-security places that data is exposed to compromise.$$),
-- 7.5 acronym (ci-a-524..527)
('ci-a-524','cissp','acronym',7,'7.5',$$NAID$$,$$National Association for Information Destruction - an independent body whose AAA certification verifies that a media-destruction vendor's process, facilities, and personnel meet recognized security standards.$$),
('ci-a-525','cissp','acronym',7,'7.5',$$RMA$$,$$Return Merchandise Authorization - the vendor-approved process for returning failed hardware for warranty repair or replacement; sensitive media should be sanitized or removed before the hardware leaves organizational control this way.$$),
('ci-a-526','cissp','acronym',7,'7.5',$$FRU$$,$$Field-Replaceable Unit - a hardware component, such as a drive or power supply, designed to be swapped out during maintenance without returning the entire system to the vendor.$$),
('ci-a-527','cissp','acronym',7,'7.5',$$ESD$$,$$Electrostatic Discharge - a static-electricity event capable of silently damaging sensitive electronic components during handling; grounding tools like a wrist strap prevent it.$$),
-- 7.5 feynman (ci-y-332..334)
('ci-y-332','cissp','feynman',7,'7.5',$$Explain why "the vendor is trustworthy" is not a sufficient reason to skip sanitizing hardware before an RMA return.$$,$$Model answer: The instinct to skip sanitization for a trusted vendor makes intuitive sense - if you trust the company, why add the extra step? But trust in the vendor's intentions doesn't actually address the real risk, which is about custody, not character. Once that failed drive leaves your building, it is physically outside your control: it passes through shipping, sits in a vendor warehouse, gets handled by warranty-processing staff who have no relationship with your organization, and ultimately may be resold, recycled, or discarded by yet another party down the vendor's own supply chain. None of those steps require the vendor itself to be untrustworthy for something to go wrong - a single dishonest warehouse employee, a lost shipment, or simply an established process that doesn't treat "failed" drives with the same care as new inventory is enough. And critically, "failed" from the server's point of view often just means the drive stopped functioning in its original system, not that its platters are unreadable to someone with forensic tools and no time pressure. Sanitizing before it leaves your custody isn't an accusation against the vendor - it's simply not betting sensitive data on every link of a supply chain you can't see or control.$$),
('ci-y-333','cissp','feynman',7,'7.5',$$Explain why decommissioned equipment awaiting destruction still needs physical security controls even though it is already scheduled to be destroyed.$$,$$Model answer: It's easy to mentally file "scheduled for destruction" under "already handled," but a schedule is just a plan for the future - it changes nothing about the equipment's actual state right now. A retired server sitting in a loading dock or an unlocked closet for the two weeks before the quarterly destruction event still has its original drives in it, and those drives still hold whatever sensitive data they held on the day the server was pulled from production. Nothing about being "scheduled" for destruction physically removes that data or restricts who can walk up and take a drive out. If anything, this window is a uniquely risky moment: the equipment has often been pulled from its normal monitored location, may no longer be actively tracked the way production assets are, and everyone assumes it's "basically already gone" and therefore stops paying attention to it - exactly the conditions that let something get lost, stolen, or scavenged without anyone noticing until the destruction event reveals a piece is missing. Treating the holding period with the same custody discipline as active equipment closes that gap.$$),
('ci-y-334','cissp','feynman',7,'7.5',$$Explain how custody controls for backup media in transit serve a different goal than chain-of-custody controls for forensic evidence, even though the mechanics - logging who handled something and when - look similar.$$,$$Model answer: Both practices produce the same kind of artifact, a documented handoff trail, which is why they're easy to lump together, but they exist to answer different questions if something goes wrong later. Chain of custody for forensic evidence exists because that evidence may eventually need to convince a court that it was collected properly and never altered - the goal is legal defensibility, and a gap in the trail can get the evidence thrown out entirely, independent of whether the data itself was ever actually compromised. Custody controls for backup media in transit, by contrast, exist to protect the confidentiality and integrity of operational data as a business asset - the goal is preventing or detecting loss, theft, or tampering of something the organization needs to keep working, not proving anything to a judge. A gap in a backup tape's transport log is a security incident to investigate and remediate; a gap in an evidence chain of custody can be a permanent, unrecoverable loss of an entire legal case. Same-looking paperwork, genuinely different stakes.$$),
-- 7.6 core (ci-f-1619..1628)
('ci-f-1619','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$What happens during the detection phase of incident management, and why must it come before anything else?$$,$$An analyst validates that a security event is a genuine incident, not a false positive or benign activity, before further action is taken. Every subsequent phase - mobilizing the team, containing damage, restoring service - depends on this initial confirmation being made correctly.$$),
('ci-f-1620','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$What happens during the response phase of incident management?$$,$$The organization formally mobilizes: activating the incident response plan, paging the incident response team, and opening a tracked case - the phase where the confirmed incident actually gets acted on for the first time.$$),
('ci-f-1621','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$What happens during the mitigation phase of incident management, and how does it differ from response?$$,$$Mitigation covers active steps taken while an incident is still unfolding to limit its scope and impact, such as isolating a compromised network segment or blocking known malicious infrastructure - it is the containment work itself, distinct from response, which is mobilizing the team in the first place.$$),
('ci-f-1622','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$What does the reporting phase of incident management cover?$$,$$All required communication about the incident - internal escalation to leadership and legal, plus any externally mandated notification to regulators or affected individuals under applicable breach-notification law, within the legally required timeframe.$$),
('ci-f-1623','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$What must be verified before the recovery phase restores a compromised system to normal operation?$$,$$That the system is actually clean - confirmed via a rebuilt image or verified backups showing no attacker tooling or backdoor remains - since reconnecting a still-compromised host to production would simply reintroduce the same problem.$$),
('ci-f-1624','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$How does the remediation phase differ from the recovery phase of incident management?$$,$$Recovery asks whether systems are back to normal operation; remediation asks whether the underlying cause has actually been fixed so the same incident cannot recur - a system can be fully recovered and running while the vulnerability that caused the incident is still unpatched, which is why remediation is a distinct, later phase.$$),
('ci-f-1625','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$What happens during the lessons learned phase, and why does it matter even after an incident is fully closed?$$,$$The team holds a structured review, after closure, to identify what worked, what fell short, and updates the incident response playbook accordingly - skipping this phase means the organization is likely to repeat the same avoidable mistakes the next time a similar incident occurs.$$),
('ci-f-1626','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$Why do organizations classify incidents into severity tiers rather than responding identically to every incident?$$,$$Tiered severity ensures response urgency and resources scale with actual business impact - a single blocked phishing email and active data exfiltration from a production database warrant very different levels of escalation, and treating them identically would either waste resources or under-react to a genuinely severe event.$$),
('ci-f-1627','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$What does an incident commander do during a major incident?$$,$$Coordinates the overall response and decision-making across all workstreams - technical investigation, legal, and communications - keeping them aligned, without necessarily performing hands-on technical work themselves.$$),
('ci-f-1628','cissp','core',7,'7.6',$$What is the primary benefit of maintaining predefined incident response playbooks for common incident types?$$,$$They speed up and standardize response by providing tested, repeatable steps for known incident patterns, such as ransomware or business email compromise, reducing how much the team must improvise under the pressure of a live event.$$),
-- 7.6 acronym (ci-a-528..531)
('ci-a-528','cissp','acronym',7,'7.6',$$CSIRT$$,$$Computer Security Incident Response Team - the dedicated team responsible for detecting, responding to, and coordinating an organization's handling of security incidents.$$),
('ci-a-529','cissp','acronym',7,'7.6',$$IRP$$,$$Incident Response Plan - the documented procedures an organization activates during the response phase, defining roles, escalation paths, and expected actions across the incident lifecycle.$$),
('ci-a-530','cissp','acronym',7,'7.6',$$RCA$$,$$Root Cause Analysis - the investigative work performed during remediation to identify the underlying condition that allowed an incident to occur, so it can be permanently fixed rather than just contained.$$),
('ci-a-531','cissp','acronym',7,'7.6',$$IOC$$,$$Indicator of Compromise - an observable artifact (a malicious IP, file hash, or unusual outbound connection) that trained detection during the detection phase looks for as evidence an incident has actually occurred.$$),
-- 7.6 feynman (ci-y-335..336)
('ci-y-335','cissp','feynman',7,'7.6',$$Explain why recovery and remediation are easy to confuse, using a concrete example to show how they differ.$$,$$Model answer: Imagine a company gets hit with an incident because an internet-facing server was running a web application with a known, unpatched vulnerability. The response team contains the damage, rebuilds the server from a clean image, verifies no backdoor remains, and brings it back online serving traffic normally again by the end of the day. From the outside, everything looks fixed - the website is back up, customers can use it, the dashboard shows green. That's recovery: the system is functioning normally again. But here's the trap - if the team stops there, the exact same unpatched vulnerability that let the attacker in the first time is still sitting on that freshly rebuilt server, because rebuilding from an image just restores the system to a known state, it doesn't necessarily fix what was wrong with that state. Remediation is the separate step of actually identifying that root cause, patching it everywhere it exists, and maybe revising the vulnerability-scanning process that should have caught it originally. A system can be 100% recovered - up, green, serving customers - while still being just as vulnerable to the exact same attack happening again tomorrow, which is precisely why treating recovery as "done" without also completing remediation is such a common and costly mistake.$$),
('ci-y-336','cissp','feynman',7,'7.6',$$Explain why the lessons-learned phase matters even after an incident is fully closed and every system is back to normal.$$,$$Model answer: Once systems are recovered and the root cause is remediated, it feels like the incident is genuinely over - there's a natural pull to close the ticket and move on to the next fire. But treating "the systems are fixed" as the finish line misses that the incident also just generated an enormous amount of information about how the organization actually performs under real pressure, which is very different from how it performs on paper. Maybe the playbook said to notify legal within thirty minutes, but it actually took two hours because nobody could find the current contact list. Maybe two team members did the same containment step independently because the plan didn't clearly assign it to one owner. None of that shows up by looking at whether the technical fix worked - it only shows up if someone deliberately steps back and asks "how did the process itself hold up?" Skipping that review doesn't just waste a learning opportunity; it means the exact same friction, gaps, and confusion get quietly carried forward into the next incident, un-diagnosed, guaranteed to slow the team down again in exactly the same way.$$),
-- 7.7 core (ci-f-1629..1638)
('ci-f-1629','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$How does behavioral (heuristic) malware detection differ from signature-based detection, and why does an EPP need both?$$,$$Signature-based detection matches files against a database of already-known malware and is fast and reliable for known threats, but blind to anything new. Behavioral detection instead flags suspicious runtime activity patterns, such as mass file encryption or unusual outbound calls, catching never-before-seen malware that has no matching signature yet.$$),
('ci-f-1630','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$What can an EDR tool do that traditional antivirus cannot?$$,$$EDR continuously records process, file, and network activity on each endpoint, letting an analyst later search that history to reconstruct exactly how an attacker moved, and it supports active remote response, such as isolating a compromised endpoint - capabilities well beyond antivirus's point-in-time blocking of known-bad files.$$),
('ci-f-1631','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$What is application allowlisting, and how does its default posture differ from blocklisting?$$,$$Allowlisting denies everything by default except an explicitly approved, typically digitally signed set of applications. Blocklisting instead permits everything by default except items matching a known-bad list - meaning allowlisting can stop malware it has never seen before, since anything unapproved is blocked regardless.$$),
('ci-f-1632','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$In what kind of environment does application allowlisting fit especially well, and why?$$,$$Environments where the legitimate set of applications is small, stable, and well-known, such as kiosk terminals or single-purpose servers - the default-deny posture is manageable to maintain there and reliably catches unknown threats, whereas it becomes far more operationally burdensome on general-purpose workstations where users need to run new software constantly.$$),
('ci-f-1633','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$What is sandboxing (detonation analysis), and why is it used before delivering an email attachment?$$,$$Sandboxing executes a suspicious file inside an isolated, instrumented environment and observes its runtime behavior before it ever reaches a real user, catching malware crafted to evade static signature matching by judging it on what it actually does rather than what it matches.$$),
('ci-f-1634','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$What is a honeytoken, and how does it differ from a honeypot?$$,$$A honeytoken is a decoy piece of data or credential, such as a fake database record, planted inside a real production system - any use of it is a near-certain sign of unauthorized access. A honeypot, by contrast, is an entire decoy system built to attract and study attacker activity in isolation from production.$$),
('ci-f-1635','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$Why does a passively-deployed honeypot sit on firmer legal ground than one that actively lures attackers?$$,$$Passively waiting to be attacked (enticement) is generally legally defensible, while actively luring or encouraging someone who would not otherwise have attacked (entrapment-like inducement) raises legal and evidentiary risk that can undermine both the deployment's legality and any resulting evidence.$$),
('ci-f-1636','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$Why must IDS/IPS signatures and threat-intelligence feeds be kept current as an ongoing operational task?$$,$$A detection tool is only as current as the intelligence feeding it - without regular updates, an IPS effectively freezes at the threat landscape that existed when it was deployed, growing steadily less effective as new attack techniques emerge that it was never taught to recognize.$$),
('ci-f-1637','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$Why do firewall rule bases need periodic review and audit, even after being correctly configured initially?$$,$$Rule bases accumulate cruft over time as systems are decommissioned and temporary troubleshooting rules are never removed - each forgotten, overly permissive rule is a small, silent expansion of the attack surface that only a periodic audit is positioned to catch and clean up.$$),
('ci-f-1638','cissp','core',7,'7.7',$$What operational benefit comes from an EPP automatically distributing a block for a newly confirmed malicious file hash across the entire managed fleet?$$,$$It closes the gap between detection on one endpoint and protection everywhere else - instead of waiting for a human analyst to manually push the same block to every machine, the fleet-wide propagation shrinks the window during which other endpoints remain exposed to the same threat.$$),
-- 7.7 acronym (ci-a-532..535)
('ci-a-532','cissp','acronym',7,'7.7',$$EPP$$,$$Endpoint Protection Platform - preventative endpoint software combining signature, heuristic, and behavioral detection layers to block malware before it executes.$$),
('ci-a-533','cissp','acronym',7,'7.7',$$EDR$$,$$Endpoint Detection and Response - endpoint software that continuously records activity and enables investigation and active remote response, going beyond an EPP's block-on-detection function.$$),
('ci-a-534','cissp','acronym',7,'7.7',$$WDAC$$,$$Windows Defender Application Control - an operating-system-level allowlisting mechanism that enforces which applications and drivers are permitted to run based on defined policy.$$),
('ci-a-535','cissp','acronym',7,'7.7',$$YARA$$,$$Yet Another Recursive Acronym - a customizable pattern-matching rule format used by analysts to identify and classify files as matching known malware families during detection and threat hunting.$$),
-- 7.7 feynman (ci-y-337..339)
('ci-y-337','cissp','feynman',7,'7.7',$$Explain, with an example, why an organization needs both signature-based and behavioral-based malware detection rather than relying on just one.$$,$$Model answer: Picture a large fleet of endpoints facing two very different kinds of threats on the same day: a widely circulating, well-documented piece of commodity malware that every vendor's threat lab has already analyzed, and a custom-built tool an attacker wrote specifically to target this one organization, that has literally never existed anywhere else before. Signature-based detection handles the first case beautifully - it's fast, has essentially no false positives, and barely uses any resources, because it's just comparing a hash against a known-bad list. But that same mechanism is structurally incapable of catching the second case: there is no signature for something nobody has ever seen or catalogued, so a purely signature-based tool would let it walk right through. Behavioral detection flips the question from "have I seen this exact file before?" to "is this program doing something a legitimate program has no business doing?" - like suddenly encrypting thousands of files or reaching out to an address nobody recognizes - which lets it catch the custom malware precisely because it judges conduct, not identity. Relying on only one leaves you either drowning in resource-hungry behavioral analysis for threats a cheap signature check would have caught instantly, or blind to anything genuinely new - which is why real endpoint protection layers both together.$$),
('ci-y-338','cissp','feynman',7,'7.7',$$Explain the difference between a honeypot and a honeytoken, and give an example of when you would use each.$$,$$Model answer: Both are decoys designed to catch someone who has no business being there, but they differ in scale and placement. A honeypot is an entire fake system - maybe a server that looks like a poorly-secured database, sitting off in its own isolated segment with no real data or production purpose, existing purely so that any interaction with it at all is suspicious by definition. You'd deploy one when you want to actively study attacker behavior in a contained space, or add a tripwire to a part of your network an attacker might explore during reconnaissance. A honeytoken is much smaller and lives inside your actual production systems rather than off to the side - a single fake customer record with a bogus API key embedded in a real database table, alongside thousands of genuine records. You'd use a honeytoken when you specifically want to detect unauthorized access to production data itself, since a honeypot sitting in an isolated segment would never even see an attacker who goes straight for the real database. In short: honeypots are for observing attackers in a space built just for them; honeytokens are for catching attackers who are already inside the space you actually care about protecting.$$),
('ci-y-339','cissp','feynman',7,'7.7',$$Explain why application allowlisting is harder to maintain operationally than blocklisting, despite being more secure in principle.$$,$$Model answer: On paper, allowlisting sounds strictly better - deny everything except what you've explicitly approved, and unknown malware simply can't run because it was never approved in the first place. The catch shows up the moment you try to actually operate it at scale. A blocklist only needs updating when a new threat is discovered - the vast, ever-changing landscape of legitimate software just quietly works, because the default is "allowed." An allowlist inverts that: every single legitimate application, every version update, every new script a developer needs to run, every internal tool someone builds, has to be explicitly identified and added before it can function at all - and if the list falls even slightly behind reality, you get a flood of help-desk tickets from people whose completely legitimate work suddenly stopped running. On a small, static environment like a kiosk that only ever runs three fixed applications, that maintenance burden is trivial. On a general-purpose workstation fleet where developers are constantly installing new tools, it becomes a constant, resource-intensive administrative tax - which is exactly why the more secure option in principle isn't automatically the right operational choice everywhere.$$);
